# Arduino Bluetooth Controlled 4WD RC Car

An open-source, Arduino-powered 4-wheel drive (4WD) robotic car controlled via Bluetooth using an Android smartphone app or custom Bluetooth terminal.

---

## 🛠️ Components Used

| Component | Quantity | Notes |
|---|:---:|---|
| **Arduino Uno** | 1 | Microcontroller Board |
| **HC-05 Bluetooth Module** | 1 | Wireless Serial Communication |
| **L298N Dual H-Bridge Motor Driver** | 1 | Motor Driver Module |
| **DC Geared Motors** | 4 | 3V-6V TT Motors |
| **RC Car Chassis** | 1 | 4WD Acrylic Chassis Frame |
| **Battery Pack** | 1 | 7.4V - 12V (e.g., 2x 18650 Li-ion Batteries) |
| **Resistors** | 2 | 1kΩ and 2kΩ (for logic voltage divider) |
| **Jumper Wires & Switch** | As required | Male-to-Female & Male-to-Male |

---

## 🔌 Pin Connections & Circuit Wiring

### 1. HC-05 Bluetooth Module

> ⚠️ **Important Note on 3.3V Logic:** The HC-05 RX pin operates on **3.3V logic**, whereas the Arduino digital outputs supply **5V**. Although the Arduino board features a 3.3V power pin, that pin only provides power output and **cannot** lower signal voltages. To prevent damaging the HC-05 module over time, use a simple resistor voltage divider on the RX line.

| HC-05 Pin | Arduino Uno | Details / Connection Notes |
|---|---|---|
| **VCC** | 5V | Power supply (HC-05 has onboard 5V to 3.3V regulator) |
| **GND** | GND | Common Ground |
| **TX** | Pin 10 | SoftwareSerial RX (Receives 3.3V signal into Arduino safely) |
| **RX** | Pin 11 | SoftwareSerial TX (**Connect via Voltage Divider** below) |

#### Voltage Divider Wiring for HC-05 RX:
```
Arduino Pin 11 (5V TX) ─── [ 1kΩ Resistor ] ───┬─── HC-05 RX Pin (3.3V Logic)
                                               │
                                       [ 2kΩ Resistor ]
                                               │
                                              GND
```

---

### 2. L298N Motor Driver Pins

> 📌 **PWM Pin Selection:** `ENB` has been configured on **Pin 9** (instead of Pin 8) because Pin 8 on the Arduino Uno does not support hardware PWM speed control.

| L298N Pin | Arduino Uno Pin | Function |
|---|---|---|
| **ENA** | Pin 3 | Left Motors Speed Control (PWM) |
| **IN1** | Pin 4 | Left Motors Direction Control |
| **IN2** | Pin 5 | Left Motors Direction Control |
| **ENB** | Pin 9 | Right Motors Speed Control (PWM) |
| **IN3** | Pin 6 | Right Motors Direction Control |
| **IN4** | Pin 7 | Right Motors Direction Control |

---

### 3. Motor Wiring (4 Motors to 2 Channels)

Because the L298N module provides terminal blocks for two motor output channels (Channel A and Channel B), the 4 motors are wired in **parallel pairs** based on their side:

| L298N Terminal | Connected Motors | Wiring Instructions |
|---|---|---|
| **OUT1 & OUT2** | **Left Motors** (Front & Rear) | Join both Left (+) wires to **OUT1**; join both Left (-) wires to **OUT2**. |
| **OUT3 & OUT4** | **Right Motors** (Front & Rear) | Join both Right (+) wires to **OUT3**; join both Right (-) wires to **OUT4**. |

*Note: If a specific motor spins in reverse when testing, simply swap its individual positive and negative wires in the screw terminal block.*

---

### 4. Power Supply Connections

* **Battery Positive (+):** Connected to L298N **12V / Power Terminal**.
* **Battery Negative (-):** Connected to L298N **GND Terminal** AND Arduino **GND** (*Common ground is required for stable communication*).
* **Arduino Power:** Can be powered via the L298N 5V output to Arduino **5V/Vin**, or via a separate 9V battery/USB power source.

---

## 🎮 Bluetooth Control Commands

| Command Character | Action | Left Motors | Right Motors |
|:---:|---|---|---|
| **`F`** | Forward | Forward | Forward |
| **`B`** | Backward | Backward | Backward |
| **`L`** | Turn Left (Spin on spot) | Backward | Forward |
| **`R`** | Turn Right (Spin on spot) | Forward | Backward |
| **`S`** | Stop | Off (0 PWM) | Off (0 PWM) |

*Default motor speed is set to **200/255** (~78% duty cycle) using PWM.*

